<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ella Baker</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1903">1903</a>–<a href="/years/1986">1986</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ella Baker was an American civil rights and human rights activist, a largely behind-the-scenes organiser whose career spanned more than five decades. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naacp">NAACP</a>'s director of branches from 1943, she was later the first staff member of the Southern Christian Leadership Conference. In 1960 she had the SCLC invite student sit-in leaders to Shaw University, where they founded the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee, and she urged them to stay independent of the older leaders. She held that &quot;strong people don't need strong leaders&quot; and promoted grassroots organising.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ella-baker.jpg" alt="Ella Baker" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ella_Baker_(1903-1986)_circa_1944.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
